# Agentic AI Workbench - Cursor Rules
# Based on: AI Agents in Action by Micheal Lanham
# Owner: Edwin Salguero, Parallel LLC

## Project Context
This is a production-grade agentic AI development workbench. It implements
the core frameworks from "AI Agents in Action": agent profiles, tool use,
memory/knowledge (RAG), reasoning (ReAct, CoT, Tree of Thought), planning
(sequential/stepwise), evaluation, feedback loops, behavior trees, and
multi-agent orchestration.

## Architecture Principles
- Every agent has a PROFILE (persona + goals + constraints)
- Every agent has MEMORY (conversational, semantic, episodic, procedural)
- Every agent has TOOLS (actions it can take on the outside world)
- Every agent has REASONING (how it thinks through problems)
- Every agent has PLANNING (how it sequences actions)
- Every agent has EVALUATION (how it scores its own output)
- Every agent has FEEDBACK (how it improves over time)

## Code Standards
- Python 3.11+ with type hints on all functions
- Use pydantic for all data models and configs
- Use async/await for all LLM calls and I/O
- Environment variables for all secrets (never hardcode API keys)
- All agents must be testable with mock LLM responses
- Use structured logging (structlog) not print statements
- No em dashes in any output or documentation

## File Organization
- agents/profiles/ -> YAML agent profile definitions
- agents/tools/ -> Tool implementations agents can use
- agents/actions/ -> Complex action sequences
- memory/ -> RAG, vector stores, memory compression
- reasoning/ -> ReAct loops, CoT chains, evaluation rubrics
- planning/ -> Sequential and stepwise planners
- multiagent/ -> CrewAI and custom orchestration
- prompts/ -> Prompt templates, flows, personas
- config/ -> Environment and model configuration
- tests/ -> Mirrors src structure with test_ prefix

## Naming Conventions
- Agent profiles: snake_case.yaml (e.g., research_analyst.yaml)
- Python modules: snake_case.py
- Classes: PascalCase
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- Prompt templates: {purpose}_{version}.jinja2

## When Writing Agent Code
1. Always start with the agent profile YAML
2. Define tools before writing agent logic
3. Include evaluation rubrics for any agent output
4. Add feedback collection to every agent interaction
5. Use the ReAct loop pattern for complex tasks
6. Implement memory persistence for long-running agents

## When Debugging
- Check agent profile constraints first
- Verify tool permissions and API connectivity
- Inspect the reasoning trace (ReAct thought/action/observation)
- Review memory retrieval relevance scores
- Check planning step completion status

## Dependencies
- anthropic or openai for LLM access
- langchain + langchain-community for RAG pipelines
- chromadb for vector storage
- crewai for multi-agent orchestration
- pydantic for data validation
- jinja2 for prompt templates
- structlog for logging
- fastapi + uvicorn for serving agents
- streamlit for rapid prototyping UI
